   "source": [
    "## What This Notebook Does and Does Not Show\n",
    "\n",
    "**Shows:** the proposed cross-attention fusion mechanism is learnable end-to-end on a controlled synthetic task; the full training/evaluation/ablation pipeline executes correctly and produces internally consistent results across configurations and seeds; a naive concatenation baseline is a genuinely competitive point of comparison at this scale, not a straw man.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Does not show:** that MMCA-Detect detects real adversarial attacks on real sensor data; that cross-attention fusion outperforms naive concatenation (the opposite trend appears on F1/FPR in this synthetic run); that the architecture meets the latency, power, or accuracy targets of the paper's Section I-B on real automotive hardware; or that the cross-modal-consistency mechanism (paper Section III-D) functions as hypothesized, since independently-sampled synthetic modalities cannot exercise it (see paper Section VII-B).\n",
    "\n",
    "The real-world validation protocol this feasibility check is meant to precede \u2014 real datasets (KITTI, nuScenes, Waymo Open), real baselines, real automotive hardware \u2014 is specified in full in the paper's Section V-F and has not been executed.\n"
   ]
  }
